The DC CLOS BGP fabric was examined in full — all five FRR routers (two leaves, two spines, one super-spine), both host endpoints, and the complete BGP and IP routing state. Every BGP session is established and stable (all up for ~12 minutes with consistent keepalive exchange and no NOTIFICATIONs or session resets). All prefix advertisements are propagating correctly: leaf_router_0_0 (AS 65200) originates 10.0.0.0/24, leaf_router_0_1 (AS 65201) originates 10.0.1.0/24, and both are visible in the FIB of every transit router. End-to-end ICMP from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) completes with 0% packet loss over five packets, TTL=61 confirming the expected three-hop data path (leaf → spine → leaf). IP forwarding is enabled on both leaf routers. Host default gateways are correctly configured. The only "unusual" observation — spine_router_0_0 accepting zero prefixes from super_spine_router_0 — is expected: super_spine's best paths for both leaf subnets were originally learned through spine_0_0 itself (AS path contains 65100), so when re-advertised they are correctly rejected by AS-path loop prevention; spine_0_0 obtains both prefixes via its direct leaf connections instead. There is no current fault, misconfiguration, missing route, interface error, or packet drop anywhere in the fabric. The user's reported outage most likely coincided with the BGP convergence window immediately following last night's maintenance restart (FRR startup logs confirm all sessions came up together at the same moment, consistent with a daemon or lab restart); convergence completes in under two seconds, after which connectivity is fully restored. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===